1
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,175
[MILVERTON]
Is that...?

2
00:00:11,217 --> 00:00:16,180
If I recall correctly, his
name is William James Moriarty.

3
00:00:16,222 --> 00:00:18,891
Second son of
the Moriarty family

4
00:00:18,932 --> 00:00:21,727
and young mathematics prodigy.

5
00:00:21,768 --> 00:00:23,020
Interesting.

6
00:00:23,062 --> 00:00:26,607
So that is the man with
whom I am competing.

7
00:00:26,649 --> 00:00:30,027
I see. I must thoroughly
investigate him at once

8
00:00:30,069 --> 00:00:32,572
and add him to the list.

9
00:00:32,613 --> 00:00:34,448
After all,

10
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:40,413
I, Charles Augustus Milverton,
am thorough in my work.

11
00:02:13,339 --> 00:02:15,424
[WILLIAM]
Okay, so this time.

12
00:02:15,466 --> 00:02:18,885
There is a certain number that
goes into both these boxes.

13
00:02:18,927 --> 00:02:20,596
Do you know what it is?

14
00:02:20,638 --> 00:02:21,597
Uhh...

15
00:02:21,639 --> 00:02:23,557
It's impossible to have
12 of one thing

16
00:02:23,599 --> 00:02:24,725
and 20 of something else

17
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:27,936
and for them to add up
the same number.

18
00:02:27,978 --> 00:02:30,773
Perhaps I should have
started with an easier problem.

19
00:02:30,815 --> 00:02:32,858
The correct answer's...

20
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:36,529
What? But that
doesn't make sense!

21
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:38,989
[WILLIAM] It does if
they're units of money.

22
00:02:39,906 --> 00:02:42,117
[WILLIAM]
Twelve pence is one shilling.

23
00:02:42,159 --> 00:02:44,411
Twenty shillings is one pound.

24
00:02:44,453 --> 00:02:48,915
Which means that
one pound is 240 pence.

25
00:02:48,957 --> 00:02:51,377
Now, let me see.
That would get you...

26
00:02:51,418 --> 00:02:54,129
Right, 240 loaves of bread.

27
00:02:54,171 --> 00:02:55,756
What?! We could eat like kings!

28
00:02:55,798 --> 00:02:57,508
Really?!

29
00:02:57,550 --> 00:02:59,635
[BOY 18A]
So if we had a hundred pounds...

30
00:02:59,677 --> 00:03:01,220
[BOY 18B]
That'd be...

31
00:03:01,261 --> 00:03:03,514
[BOY 18B, BOY 18A]
...24,000 loaves!

32
00:03:03,556 --> 00:03:04,890
That's more than
I could eat in a lifetime!

33
00:03:04,931 --> 00:03:08,602
[BOYS laugh]

34
00:03:11,605 --> 00:03:13,899
[CHILDREN grunt]

35
00:03:14,983 --> 00:03:17,570
[BOY 18B]
This is all we get again?

36
00:03:17,611 --> 00:03:19,613
Here. You can have
mine if you'd like.

37
00:03:19,655 --> 00:03:22,282
I'm not feeling
very hungry tonight.

38
00:03:22,324 --> 00:03:23,492
[BOY 18B]
Thank you.

39
00:03:23,534 --> 00:03:24,744
[chuckles]

40
00:03:24,785 --> 00:03:26,579
[LOUIS]
Hm?

41
00:03:30,207 --> 00:03:30,999
[sighs]

42
00:03:31,041 --> 00:03:33,669
- -[footsteps]
- -[WILLIAM] Madam.

43
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:36,422
It's you two.

44
00:03:36,463 --> 00:03:39,550
What brings you here
so late in the evening?

45
00:03:39,592 --> 00:03:42,845
We thought there might be
something troubling you.

46
00:03:42,887 --> 00:03:43,846
That perhaps someone had

47
00:03:43,888 --> 00:03:46,014
swindled the orphanage
out of its funds.

48
00:03:46,056 --> 00:03:46,682
[gasps]

49
00:03:46,724 --> 00:03:48,308
[WILLIAM]
Are we wrong?

50
00:03:49,852 --> 00:03:51,978
But how could you know that?

51
00:03:53,313 --> 00:03:55,733
Similarly to the almshouse,

52
00:03:55,775 --> 00:03:58,694
we're supported through
donations made by the nobility,

53
00:03:58,736 --> 00:04:01,614
as I'm sure you're aware.

54
00:04:01,655 --> 00:04:03,073
Last month,

55
00:04:03,115 --> 00:04:07,620
an aristocrat by the name
of Lord Baxter visited me.

56
00:04:07,661 --> 00:04:10,873
[BAXTER] I require a meager
amount, a mere 300 pounds,

57
00:04:10,915 --> 00:04:14,042
and then construction on
the orphanage can begin.

58
00:04:14,084 --> 00:04:16,545
The other day you received
a generous donation,

59
00:04:16,587 --> 00:04:18,171
or so I've heard.

60
00:04:18,213 --> 00:04:20,549
I'm begging you. Show mercy.

61
00:04:20,591 --> 00:04:24,303
For the sake of the orphans,
may I please borrow the money?

62
00:04:24,344 --> 00:04:27,473
I finally found the perfect plot
on which to build the orphanage.

63
00:04:27,514 --> 00:04:29,433
But it'll be sold
to someone else

64
00:04:29,475 --> 00:04:32,478
if I do not put
a deposit down at once.

65
00:04:32,519 --> 00:04:35,522
But the children here...

66
00:04:35,564 --> 00:04:37,190
Trust me! If something happens

67
00:04:37,232 --> 00:04:39,234
that prevents me
from paying you back,

68
00:04:39,276 --> 00:04:42,404
I offer myself in exchange.

69
00:04:42,446 --> 00:04:45,031
[WOMAN 18A] The 300 wasn't
my own personal funds,

70
00:04:45,073 --> 00:04:48,452
so I knew up front that it would
be wrong for me to lend it out.

71
00:04:48,494 --> 00:04:51,288
But he gave me a
certificate of borrowing.

72
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:56,042
And I thought a nobleman's
word was absolute, so I...

73
00:04:57,419 --> 00:05:00,046
However, the appointed
day came and went

74
00:05:00,088 --> 00:05:02,174
and there was no sign
he would pay me back.

75
00:05:02,215 --> 00:05:04,593
Or of the orphanage being built.

76
00:05:06,219 --> 00:05:08,388
[WILLIAM]
If you wouldn't mind, madam,

77
00:05:08,430 --> 00:05:11,183
I'd like to examine
the certificate.

78
00:05:16,021 --> 00:05:18,398
[BAXTER]
Mmhm.

79
00:05:31,244 --> 00:05:34,331
[gasps] You poor hungry waifs.

80
00:05:34,373 --> 00:05:36,876
Here, buy some food.

81
00:05:36,917 --> 00:05:40,253
[WILLIAM] We have no need
for your alms, Mister Baxter.

82
00:05:40,295 --> 00:05:41,547
The fact is

83
00:05:41,588 --> 00:05:44,675
we're here on behalf of
the madam from our orphanage.

84
00:05:44,717 --> 00:05:47,219
I trust you know of
whom I'm speaking.

85
00:05:51,056 --> 00:05:52,057
[BAXTER]
The madam,

86
00:05:52,098 --> 00:05:54,476
is she feeling under
the weather, perhaps?

87
00:05:54,518 --> 00:05:57,145
[LOUIS] Her hands are full
tending to the orphanage.

88
00:05:57,187 --> 00:05:59,023
Poor woman.

89
00:05:59,064 --> 00:06:01,233
I assume this
concerns the 300 pounds

90
00:06:01,274 --> 00:06:03,944
I had borrowed
from her last we met.

91
00:06:03,986 --> 00:06:05,320
Unfortunately,

92
00:06:05,362 --> 00:06:08,198
construction of the
orphanage has not proceeded.

93
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:10,034
There's also the
matter of finding someone

94
00:06:10,075 --> 00:06:13,537
to manage the project.
I'm afraid I'm out of my depth.

95
00:06:13,579 --> 00:06:15,288
You're mistaken.

96
00:06:15,330 --> 00:06:16,331
Huh?

97
00:06:16,373 --> 00:06:17,541
[WILLIAM]
We're not here to inquire about

98
00:06:17,583 --> 00:06:20,002
how construction is progressing.

99
00:06:20,044 --> 00:06:22,922
Rather, we're here to collect
the money you borrowed,

100
00:06:22,963 --> 00:06:24,840
if you'd kindly return it.

101
00:06:24,882 --> 00:06:27,760
Beg your pardon,
but this coming month

102
00:06:27,801 --> 00:06:30,429
I will be earning profit
through my trading.

103
00:06:30,470 --> 00:06:34,349
Once this happens, I intend
to repay the money post haste.

104
00:06:34,391 --> 00:06:36,977
[WILLIAM] While I appreciate
that, the truth is

105
00:06:37,019 --> 00:06:40,313
we don't even have enough
to afford food at the moment.

106
00:06:40,355 --> 00:06:43,108
If you do not repay
the money immediately,

107
00:06:43,149 --> 00:06:45,945
our orphanage will
be unable to operate.

108
00:06:45,986 --> 00:06:48,697
[BAXTER] The importance of
the money is not lost on me.

109
00:06:48,739 --> 00:06:52,910
However, I cannot return
what I do not have.

110
00:06:52,952 --> 00:06:54,119
[BAXTER grunts]

111
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:56,914
[WILLIAM]
I understand. In that case,

112
00:06:56,956 --> 00:06:59,792
what if we were to assume
your debt with the madam

113
00:06:59,833 --> 00:07:02,294
and put up the 300 pounds?

114
00:07:04,964 --> 00:07:06,048
[BAXTER]
Uh.

115
00:07:06,090 --> 00:07:09,051
- -[coins jingle]
- -[gasps]

116
00:07:09,093 --> 00:07:12,220
[WILLIAM] As you can see,
we are good for it.

117
00:07:12,262 --> 00:07:13,513
That's astonishing.

118
00:07:13,555 --> 00:07:16,182
How did a child come
into such wealth?

119
00:07:16,224 --> 00:07:18,310
[WILLIAM]
I've consulted with many adults

120
00:07:18,351 --> 00:07:21,814
and saved what they've
rewarded me over time.

121
00:07:21,855 --> 00:07:23,023
Furthermore,

122
00:07:23,065 --> 00:07:26,318
after hearing of your
unfortunate circumstances...

123
00:07:27,778 --> 00:07:30,030
[WILLIAM] ...it sounds as
though you could use more.

124
00:07:30,072 --> 00:07:33,325
I will personally lend
you an additional 300.

125
00:07:33,366 --> 00:07:36,536
I hope this will help avoid
future delays with construction.

126
00:07:36,578 --> 00:07:39,706
[BAXTER gasps]
You're saying

127
00:07:39,748 --> 00:07:42,084
you're prepared to
lend me 600 pounds

128
00:07:42,126 --> 00:07:45,712
and my debt to the orphanage
will be written off?

129
00:07:45,754 --> 00:07:48,340
We have a deal!
I accept your offer.

130
00:07:48,381 --> 00:07:49,967
You name the conditions!

131
00:07:50,009 --> 00:07:51,802
Will the same terms
as the contract

132
00:07:51,844 --> 00:07:54,179
I had with the madam suffice?

133
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:55,514
Yes.

134
00:07:55,555 --> 00:07:59,143
But I'll only accept the same
conditions, to the letter.

135
00:07:59,184 --> 00:08:01,854
Your repayment date will be
on the first of next month.

136
00:08:01,895 --> 00:08:03,897
[BAXTER]
All right, that settles it.

137
00:08:03,939 --> 00:08:06,650
Let's take this to
the notary right away!

138
00:08:09,736 --> 00:08:11,905
[LOUIS] Here is the 300
pounds we're lending you

139
00:08:11,947 --> 00:08:14,240
after taking out the
amount owed to the madam.

140
00:08:14,282 --> 00:08:16,326
That concludes our business.

141
00:08:16,368 --> 00:08:18,244
Well, until the
first of next month.

142
00:08:18,286 --> 00:08:20,664
I'm certain I'll be
able to repay you.

143
00:08:20,706 --> 00:08:22,958
[WILLIAM]
I have no doubt.

144
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,043
If you'll excuse us,

145
00:08:24,084 --> 00:08:26,795
we must see this money
gets delivered safely.

146
00:08:34,678 --> 00:08:39,725
[BAXTER cackling]

147
00:08:41,810 --> 00:08:45,438
[BAXTER] Once again, I have
enough to freely amuse myself.

148
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:47,440
The have-nots give
what little they possess

149
00:08:47,482 --> 00:08:51,195
to the cultured elite--
that's the way of the world.

150
00:08:51,236 --> 00:08:53,197
If you wish to curse something,

151
00:08:53,238 --> 00:08:56,909
curse the situation
you've placed yourself in!

152
00:08:57,868 --> 00:08:59,870
[coughs]

153
00:08:59,912 --> 00:09:02,330
Brother, are you certain
it was a wise choice

154
00:09:02,372 --> 00:09:05,042
to give that man
another 300 pounds?

155
00:09:05,084 --> 00:09:07,044
Of course, I lent
it under the same

156
00:09:07,086 --> 00:09:10,296
conditions as the
madam, after all.

157
00:09:10,338 --> 00:09:12,632
Read the contract's
last provision.

158
00:09:13,884 --> 00:09:15,343
[LOUIS grunts]

159
00:09:16,636 --> 00:09:17,804
[LOUIS]
"On demand,

160
00:09:17,846 --> 00:09:21,183
we promise to take a pound
of the borrower's flesh

161
00:09:21,225 --> 00:09:23,769
if the bonder is unable
to pay a debt repayment

162
00:09:23,810 --> 00:09:26,105
at the specified date."

163
00:09:26,146 --> 00:09:28,982
Wh-Why, this contract,

164
00:09:29,024 --> 00:09:31,443
it's similar to the one in...

165
00:09:31,484 --> 00:09:33,237
That's right.

166
00:09:33,278 --> 00:09:36,531
The agreement Shylock
and Antonio entered into

167
00:09:36,573 --> 00:09:38,200
from the classic work,

168
00:09:38,242 --> 00:09:41,369
William Shakespeare's
The Merchant of Venice  .

169
00:09:41,411 --> 00:09:44,831
Apparently, he's
a lover of the arts.

170
00:09:44,873 --> 00:09:46,708
[LOUIS]
However, in the play,

171
00:09:46,750 --> 00:09:50,462
the court does not allow
Shylock to cut out his flesh.

172
00:09:50,503 --> 00:09:53,715
Nor does he even get the
money he lent returned to him.

173
00:09:53,757 --> 00:09:57,136
[WILLIAM] True, but our
ending will be different.

174
00:09:57,177 --> 00:09:58,178
[LOUIS grunts]

175
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:02,099
We'll use his avarice
to our advantage...

176
00:10:02,141 --> 00:10:05,560
...in the most famous court
case the world's ever heard.

177
00:10:17,823 --> 00:10:20,867
This court will now begin legal
proceedings into the matter

178
00:10:20,909 --> 00:10:23,203
of the default on the loan
agreement arranged by

179
00:10:23,245 --> 00:10:26,372
the plaintiff and entered
into by the defendant.

180
00:10:26,414 --> 00:10:28,541
Furthermore, the trial
will be on the record

181
00:10:28,583 --> 00:10:30,669
in accordance with statute.

182
00:10:30,710 --> 00:10:33,463
False testimony is a punishable
offence in this court,

183
00:10:33,505 --> 00:10:36,633
so make sure to choose
your words carefully.

184
00:10:36,675 --> 00:10:39,594
Both parties, are you ready?

185
00:10:40,637 --> 00:10:42,347
[JUDGE 18A]
I couldn't believe my own eyes

186
00:10:42,388 --> 00:10:43,849
when I read the complaint.

187
00:10:43,890 --> 00:10:46,935
But sure enough,
he is just a child.

188
00:10:47,936 --> 00:10:49,271
We'll begin.

189
00:10:49,313 --> 00:10:52,232
We'll start with an opening
statement from the plaintiff.

190
00:10:52,274 --> 00:10:53,859
[WILLIAM]
Yes, my lord.

191
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:55,485
As stated in my complaint,

192
00:10:55,527 --> 00:10:58,613
I, the plaintiff, am seeking
immediate repayment for

193
00:10:58,655 --> 00:11:00,615
the 600 pounds I lent

194
00:11:00,657 --> 00:11:04,828
as designated in the bond and
agreed to by the defendant.

195
00:11:04,870 --> 00:11:05,871
Very well.

196
00:11:05,912 --> 00:11:09,291
The defendant may now
make his statement.

197
00:11:09,333 --> 00:11:11,835
In regard to my
client, Lord Baxter,

198
00:11:11,877 --> 00:11:13,628
the man has zero current assets

199
00:11:13,670 --> 00:11:15,339
that can be used to
pay off this debt,

200
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:18,133
as seen in the
submitted documents.

201
00:11:18,175 --> 00:11:21,970
I think it's evident this sum
cannot immediately be paid

202
00:11:22,012 --> 00:11:24,806
as demanded by the plaintiff.

203
00:11:24,848 --> 00:11:27,642
My lord, begging your pardon,

204
00:11:27,684 --> 00:11:31,479
I would like to question the
defendant's financial situation

205
00:11:31,521 --> 00:11:32,689
with permission?

206
00:11:32,731 --> 00:11:34,233
[JUDGE 18A]
Granted.

207
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:36,526
Per these records,

208
00:11:36,568 --> 00:11:40,197
you currently do not
possess any asset holdings.

209
00:11:40,239 --> 00:11:42,157
Yet, Mister Baxter,

210
00:11:42,199 --> 00:11:46,494
you reside in a high-class
residential area.

211
00:11:46,536 --> 00:11:50,623
Had it not occurred to you
to sell your lavish estate?

212
00:11:50,665 --> 00:11:54,044
[BAXTER] As much as I'd
like to, I cannot do that.

213
00:11:54,086 --> 00:11:55,921
Your reasons being?

214
00:11:55,962 --> 00:11:57,630
I'm actually
borrowing that estate

215
00:11:57,672 --> 00:11:59,383
from a rather good
friend of mine.

216
00:11:59,424 --> 00:12:02,386
I couldn't sell it
if I wanted to.

217
00:12:02,427 --> 00:12:05,513
I see. We shall move on then.

218
00:12:05,555 --> 00:12:09,393
You also operate a trading
company, do you not?

219
00:12:09,434 --> 00:12:11,978
If you were to sell off
the ship owned by the company,

220
00:12:12,020 --> 00:12:16,566
or even the company itself,
would repayment not be possible?

221
00:12:16,608 --> 00:12:19,485
[BAXTER] I'm afraid
I cannot do that, either.

222
00:12:19,527 --> 00:12:21,529
Whilst the trading
company does exist,

223
00:12:21,571 --> 00:12:24,408
it's only on paper;
there are no buildings.

224
00:12:24,449 --> 00:12:26,492
And the freighter
I had pinned all hopes

225
00:12:26,534 --> 00:12:28,328
of seeing us through
became shipwrecked.

226
00:12:28,370 --> 00:12:30,496
It won't be returning to port.

227
00:12:30,538 --> 00:12:34,126
We are insured and the company's
losses will be covered,

228
00:12:34,167 --> 00:12:37,087
but speaking practically,
the venture's bankrupt.

229
00:12:38,422 --> 00:12:40,006
Like the estate,

230
00:12:40,048 --> 00:12:43,135
it was not included with all
your documented assets

231
00:12:43,176 --> 00:12:45,220
because it has no real value.

232
00:12:45,262 --> 00:12:48,014
[BAXTER]
Yes, that is correct.

233
00:12:48,056 --> 00:12:50,100
It's truly a pathetic tale.

234
00:12:50,142 --> 00:12:52,560
I have my title
but little else to show.

235
00:12:52,602 --> 00:12:54,479
Nothing but a fallen aristocrat,

236
00:12:54,520 --> 00:12:57,565
without a single
possession to my own name!

237
00:13:01,903 --> 00:13:05,449
Indeed. It seems that
repayment is impossible.

238
00:13:05,490 --> 00:13:07,492
What would you like to do?

239
00:13:11,037 --> 00:13:15,583
Seems I have no other option
but to give up on being repaid.

240
00:13:15,625 --> 00:13:17,419
[BAXTER chuckles]

241
00:13:17,461 --> 00:13:18,295
[WILLIAM]
As such...

242
00:13:18,337 --> 00:13:20,339
[gasps]

243
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:21,340
[WILLIAM]
...in accordance with the

244
00:13:21,381 --> 00:13:24,384
other provision of the contract,

245
00:13:24,426 --> 00:13:26,303
I will extract
one pound of flesh

246
00:13:26,345 --> 00:13:29,431
from the body of the
defendant as repayment.

247
00:13:31,683 --> 00:13:32,809
[JUDGE 18A]
Now, wait.

248
00:13:32,851 --> 00:13:35,603
You're not seriously
requesting a pound of flesh?

249
00:13:35,645 --> 00:13:40,233
I am, as is my legal right
stated in this contract

250
00:13:40,275 --> 00:13:42,944
officially issued by
a notary solicitor.

251
00:13:42,986 --> 00:13:45,530
If the terms of the
agreement are not recognized,

252
00:13:45,571 --> 00:13:48,825
the entire nation's sovereignty
must be called into question.

253
00:13:48,867 --> 00:13:50,785
[JUDGE 18A]
It is true, contractually,

254
00:13:50,827 --> 00:13:53,746
the request must be
recognized by the court.

255
00:13:53,788 --> 00:13:56,749
[LAWYER 18A, BAXTER gasp]

256
00:13:56,791 --> 00:13:58,710
[BAXTER grunts, gasps]

257
00:14:02,922 --> 00:14:06,218
[BAXTER whimpers]

258
00:14:07,927 --> 00:14:10,722
Wait just one moment!

259
00:14:10,763 --> 00:14:11,848
Yes, my lord,

260
00:14:11,890 --> 00:14:14,059
we recognise a pound
of flesh may be removed--

261
00:14:14,100 --> 00:14:16,019
that's not in question.

262
00:14:16,061 --> 00:14:17,896
But these aren't the terms!

263
00:14:17,937 --> 00:14:20,982
The contract clearly
states one pound of flesh.

264
00:14:21,024 --> 00:14:23,776
Meaning at any point
during its removal,

265
00:14:23,818 --> 00:14:27,197
the shedding of a single
drop of blood is unacceptable!

266
00:14:27,239 --> 00:14:28,907
Correct?

267
00:14:28,948 --> 00:14:31,159
[JUDGE 18A]
Hm...

268
00:14:31,201 --> 00:14:33,119
Sustained then.

269
00:14:33,161 --> 00:14:36,415
Contractually, the plaintiff may
not spill the defendant's blood

270
00:14:36,456 --> 00:14:38,875
during the act of repayment.

271
00:14:40,210 --> 00:14:42,128
No bleeding?

272
00:14:42,170 --> 00:14:44,130
Somehow, I must
cut into his flesh

273
00:14:44,172 --> 00:14:46,758
without spilling a single drop.

274
00:14:46,799 --> 00:14:49,261
Impossible.

275
00:14:49,302 --> 00:14:50,970
[BAXTER]
I've won!

276
00:14:51,012 --> 00:14:52,556
Foolish street urchin!

277
00:14:52,597 --> 00:14:54,640
The moment you put your
name on that contract,

278
00:14:54,682 --> 00:14:58,478
your fate was sealed.
My path to victory was assured!

279
00:14:58,520 --> 00:14:59,938
I also have legal proof

280
00:14:59,979 --> 00:15:02,857
you willingly forfeited
those 600 pounds to me.

281
00:15:02,899 --> 00:15:05,569
None of your demands,
not a single one,

282
00:15:05,610 --> 00:15:06,861
will be ever be met.

283
00:15:06,903 --> 00:15:09,989
There's no possible
way you'll succeed.

284
00:15:10,031 --> 00:15:11,783
[WILLIAM]
Well,

285
00:15:11,824 --> 00:15:14,911
I wish to continue my
line of inquiry then.

286
00:15:14,953 --> 00:15:16,621
[LAWYER 18A grunts]

287
00:15:16,662 --> 00:15:17,872
[WILLIAM]
As the plaintiff,

288
00:15:17,914 --> 00:15:20,666
may I call a witness
into the courtroom?

289
00:15:28,174 --> 00:15:31,386
[BAXTER] Who is this
bloke? Do I know him?

290
00:15:31,428 --> 00:15:34,180
[WILLIAM] I would like to
first question the defendant.

291
00:15:34,222 --> 00:15:35,432
Mister Baxter,

292
00:15:35,474 --> 00:15:38,768
you enjoy ordering steak
at restaurants, do you not?

293
00:15:38,810 --> 00:15:41,438
That's right, I do.

294
00:15:41,480 --> 00:15:43,523
Then if I may stand in
as the waiter,

295
00:15:43,565 --> 00:15:46,985
place your order as you
normally would, please.

296
00:15:47,026 --> 00:15:48,194
[BAXTER grunts]

297
00:15:48,236 --> 00:15:51,072
I would like you to grill
me a half-pound of beef.

298
00:15:51,114 --> 00:15:52,574
Is that sufficient?

299
00:15:52,616 --> 00:15:54,200
Yes, thank you very much.

300
00:15:54,242 --> 00:15:57,329
Moving along, I'd like
to question Mister Bremner.

301
00:15:57,370 --> 00:15:59,789
[BREMNER]
Oh. Right.

302
00:15:59,831 --> 00:16:01,291
[WILLIAM] You are a
waiter at the restaurant

303
00:16:01,333 --> 00:16:03,960
Mister Baxter frequents,
are you not?

304
00:16:04,002 --> 00:16:05,962
[BREMNER]
Yes, I am.

305
00:16:06,004 --> 00:16:08,173
[BAXTER]
So that's who he is.

306
00:16:08,214 --> 00:16:11,176
[WILLIAM] And Mister Baxter--
do you wait on him often?

307
00:16:11,217 --> 00:16:14,471
[BREMNER]
Yes. He came in just yesterday.

308
00:16:14,513 --> 00:16:17,932
I see. And did he
by chance order a steak?

309
00:16:17,974 --> 00:16:19,392
[BAXTER]
What's he playing at?

310
00:16:19,434 --> 00:16:22,228
Is the urchin implying
since I order steak often,

311
00:16:22,270 --> 00:16:25,148
I must have money?
What a laughable argument.

312
00:16:25,190 --> 00:16:27,775
Yes, and he seemed
to relish the taste

313
00:16:27,817 --> 00:16:29,861
as he always does.

314
00:16:29,902 --> 00:16:31,154
If I have this right,

315
00:16:31,196 --> 00:16:34,282
Mister Baxter always orders
his favorite size and cut

316
00:16:34,324 --> 00:16:37,452
and has no issue paying
for it. Is that correct?

317
00:16:37,494 --> 00:16:41,581
Uh. Well, yes. That's correct.

318
00:16:41,623 --> 00:16:43,291
[WILLIAM]
Well, then,

319
00:16:43,333 --> 00:16:45,877
whenever Mister Baxter
orders his steak,

320
00:16:45,918 --> 00:16:49,172
has he at any point asked
when you serve the half pound

321
00:16:49,214 --> 00:16:51,550
for the blood to be
removed from the weight?

322
00:16:51,591 --> 00:16:54,469
[BAXTER gasps]

323
00:16:54,511 --> 00:16:56,137
[BREMNER]
The weight of the blood?

324
00:16:56,179 --> 00:16:59,265
No, and I've been
a server for 20 years.

325
00:16:59,307 --> 00:17:02,018
No one's ever made
that request before.

326
00:17:02,060 --> 00:17:04,312
I trust I've made my point.

327
00:17:04,354 --> 00:17:05,480
[BAXTER]
No, you haven't!

328
00:17:05,522 --> 00:17:06,523
This whole argument is--

329
00:17:06,565 --> 00:17:08,233
[WILLIAM]
I see you're confused.

330
00:17:08,274 --> 00:17:11,152
We're establishing
your concept of "flesh."

331
00:17:11,194 --> 00:17:11,903
[LAWYER 18A grunts]

332
00:17:11,944 --> 00:17:12,820
[WILLIAM]
Earlier in court,

333
00:17:12,862 --> 00:17:15,281
your lawyer argued
per our contract,

334
00:17:15,323 --> 00:17:18,577
at no point could blood be added
to the pound of flesh removed.

335
00:17:18,618 --> 00:17:20,537
Not one drop.

336
00:17:20,579 --> 00:17:23,164
However, when the plaintiff
is a customer placing

337
00:17:23,206 --> 00:17:26,834
his regular order of steak,
his logic differs.

338
00:17:26,876 --> 00:17:28,253
For Mister Baxter,

339
00:17:28,294 --> 00:17:32,424
the concept of meat includes all
the blood contained within.

340
00:17:32,465 --> 00:17:33,717
You're wrong! I don't--

341
00:17:33,758 --> 00:17:35,176
[WILLIAM]
If I am wrong,

342
00:17:35,218 --> 00:17:37,095
then you would have asked
to remove the weight of

343
00:17:37,136 --> 00:17:40,473
the blood whenever you ordered
your traditional half pound.

344
00:17:40,515 --> 00:17:42,183
But as we know,

345
00:17:42,225 --> 00:17:45,729
you didn't--not on any occasion.

346
00:17:45,770 --> 00:17:49,190
In other words, it's been
conclusively demonstrated

347
00:17:49,232 --> 00:17:52,527
when you signed the contract,
you understood a pound of flesh

348
00:17:52,569 --> 00:17:54,320
included the
weight of the blood.

349
00:17:54,362 --> 00:17:56,114
I'll collect your debt now.

350
00:17:56,155 --> 00:17:58,824
[BAXTER] Enough!
This is-- It's chicanery!

351
00:17:58,866 --> 00:18:01,536
[WILLIAM] I believe
you're doing the chicaning.

352
00:18:01,578 --> 00:18:04,497
Your own solicitor said
the contract is valid

353
00:18:04,539 --> 00:18:07,375
and a pound of flesh
may be cut out.

354
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:09,877
[WILLIAM]
Right, my Lord?

355
00:18:09,919 --> 00:18:11,837
The lad is correct.

356
00:18:11,879 --> 00:18:13,381
This is absurd!

357
00:18:13,423 --> 00:18:15,467
The ignorant waif doesn't
even know the play on which

358
00:18:15,508 --> 00:18:17,927
the terms of the
contract are based!

359
00:18:21,973 --> 00:18:24,517
[WILLIAM]
"O, Antonio.

360
00:18:24,559 --> 00:18:28,563
If thou art prepared to accept
Shylock's knife into thy bosom,

361
00:18:28,605 --> 00:18:31,983
then lay bare thy breast!"

362
00:18:32,024 --> 00:18:33,401
When it comes to Shakespeare,

363
00:18:33,443 --> 00:18:37,905
I can recite every line from
all 40 of his works from memory.

364
00:18:37,947 --> 00:18:41,409
But that's not possible.
You're a mere guttersnipe.

365
00:18:41,451 --> 00:18:43,911
How're you...?

366
00:18:43,953 --> 00:18:45,246
[WILLIAM]
Very well.

367
00:18:45,288 --> 00:18:48,583
Now that the matter regarding
blood has been settled...

368
00:18:49,668 --> 00:18:51,711
...it's time to claim
what's rightfully mine

369
00:18:51,753 --> 00:18:54,464
and take my pound of flesh.

370
00:18:54,506 --> 00:18:57,258
No one else needs to see this.

371
00:18:57,300 --> 00:19:00,052
[BAXTER whimpering]
Wait.

372
00:19:00,094 --> 00:19:02,180
Listen to reason!

373
00:19:02,221 --> 00:19:05,057
N-No. No!

374
00:19:05,099 --> 00:19:06,184
Wait.

375
00:19:06,225 --> 00:19:07,602
[BAXTER whimpers]

376
00:19:07,644 --> 00:19:08,853
[BAXTER]
Please, wait!

377
00:19:08,894 --> 00:19:11,856
I said-- I said
wait just a second!

378
00:19:13,608 --> 00:19:16,152
[BAXTER]
I swear, it's a lie! All of it!

379
00:19:16,194 --> 00:19:19,197
The truth is the
title on my house,

380
00:19:19,238 --> 00:19:21,783
I transferred it to a friend.

381
00:19:21,825 --> 00:19:23,451
Same for the company!

382
00:19:23,493 --> 00:19:27,372
I actually own all of it!

383
00:19:27,413 --> 00:19:28,873
[BAXTER whimpers]

384
00:19:28,914 --> 00:19:31,125
Say, what would you say to half?

385
00:19:31,167 --> 00:19:33,919
Fifty percent of
everything I own!

386
00:19:33,961 --> 00:19:37,298
My property, you name it!

387
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:38,717
[WILLIAM]
Just half?

388
00:19:38,758 --> 00:19:40,593
[BAXTER whimpers]

389
00:19:41,553 --> 00:19:42,804
That won't do.

390
00:19:42,846 --> 00:19:45,931
I want all of your
worldly possessions.

391
00:19:45,973 --> 00:19:48,184
Then we'll have a deal.

392
00:19:49,310 --> 00:19:53,272
[BAXTER cries]

393
00:19:54,315 --> 00:19:57,193
[BAXTER]
I... agree.

394
00:19:59,320 --> 00:20:01,239
[MILVERTON] Following
the conclusion of the trial,

395
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:03,282
the young men donated
the vast wealth

396
00:20:03,324 --> 00:20:05,535
they obtained from
Baxter to the orphanage,

397
00:20:05,577 --> 00:20:08,120
and then disappeared.

398
00:20:08,162 --> 00:20:10,665
Upon investigating
the adopted third son

399
00:20:10,707 --> 00:20:12,709
who perished in that fire,

400
00:20:12,751 --> 00:20:15,545
I arrived at this court record.

401
00:20:15,587 --> 00:20:17,213
I have zero doubts.

402
00:20:17,255 --> 00:20:20,800
The middle brother and the boy
from the trial are the same,

403
00:20:20,842 --> 00:20:24,095
William James Moriarty.

404
00:20:24,136 --> 00:20:26,890
After the family
manor burned down,

405
00:20:26,931 --> 00:20:30,184
he took the place
of the real William.

406
00:20:30,226 --> 00:20:31,603
And judging by the fact

407
00:20:31,644 --> 00:20:34,105
that the other brothers
readily accepted him,

408
00:20:34,146 --> 00:20:38,818
one can presume that all
of the brothers are complicit.

409
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:41,863
William James Moriarty.

410
00:20:41,905 --> 00:20:44,574
[MILVERTON chuckles]
I see.

411
00:20:44,616 --> 00:20:47,535
The name of my enemy.

412
00:22:19,585 --> 00:22:21,504
[FRED]
I come bringing news.

413
00:22:21,546 --> 00:22:22,881
I went by the courthouse.

414
00:22:22,922 --> 00:22:24,131
It seems someone has taken

415
00:22:24,173 --> 00:22:26,425
the records you wanted
to be discovered.

416
00:22:27,510 --> 00:22:28,720
[FRED]
The man responsible

417
00:22:28,761 --> 00:22:31,556
is a one Charles
Augustus Milverton.

418
00:22:31,597 --> 00:22:34,433
It appears that his objective
was not to find precedent,

419
00:22:34,475 --> 00:22:37,645
but to investigate
William's past.

420
00:22:37,687 --> 00:22:39,981
Lord Milverton, you say?

421
00:22:40,023 --> 00:22:43,317
The man owns several newspapers
and advertising firms.

422
00:22:43,359 --> 00:22:46,111
He has even been nicknamed
the King of Media.

423
00:22:46,153 --> 00:22:47,572
An aristocrat, no.

424
00:22:47,613 --> 00:22:50,575
But nonetheless,
a prominent businessman.

425
00:22:50,616 --> 00:22:52,076
What is this court
record you mentioned?

426
00:22:52,117 --> 00:22:54,537
Well, back when Will was a kid,

427
00:22:54,579 --> 00:22:57,790
he took this nobleman welsher
to court and won the case.

428
00:22:57,832 --> 00:23:00,334
Why would you leave
such a thing to be found?

429
00:23:00,376 --> 00:23:03,379
[BONDE gasps]
Then he must know.

430
00:23:03,421 --> 00:23:05,339
[WILLIAM]
Everyone, listen here.

431
00:23:05,381 --> 00:23:06,591
It's become quite evident

432
00:23:06,632 --> 00:23:10,386
that Lord Milverton
has chosen to target me.

433
00:23:10,428 --> 00:23:13,347
All of you should exercise
caution regarding him.

434
00:23:14,390 --> 00:23:16,559
[WILLIAM]
Until further notice,

435
00:23:16,601 --> 00:23:19,311
you are to consider
him our primary foe.

436
00:23:21,647 --> 00:23:23,608
Eliminate him.

437
00:23:32,408 --> 00:23:34,827
[HOLMES] Next time on
Moriarty the Patriot  :

438
00:23:34,869 --> 00:23:37,789
"The White Knight
of London, Act One."
